    "classic_presentation": "ONSET: SUDDEN onset within hours to days after starting new medication. LOCATION: GENERALIZED or localized to specific areas. DURATION: Develops over hours to days, may persist for days to weeks. CHARACTER: ERYTHEMATOUS (red), PRURITIC (itchy), may be MACULOPAPULAR (flat and raised) or VESICULAR (blistering). AGGRAVATING: SCRATCHING, heat, stress. RELIEVING: DISCONTINUING offending drug, antihistamines, topical steroids. TIMING: Develops within hours to days of drug exposure. SEVERITY: 4-8/10 itching, moderate to severe. ASSOCIATED SYMPTOMS: SEVERE ITCHING (pruritus), erythema, may have fever, may have systemic symptoms (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea). KEY POSITIVES: Erythema, pruritus, recent medication history, positive drug challenge. KEY NEGATIVES: No fever >102\u00b0F (rules out serious drug reaction), no systemic symptoms (unless severe).",
